description:
Maybe I'm doing it wrong, but I find myself writing this:

````r
cleanup_pool <- function(db_pool,conn){
  if(db_pool$valid){
      while(db_pool$counters$taken > 0) poolReturn(conn)
      poolClose(db_pool) # Close the DB pool
  }
}
````

This could be simplified if `conn <- poolCheckout(db_pool)` was given a pointer(`.Alias`?) to the `db_pool`.  then we could simplify and kill two birds with one stone with `cleanup_pool(conn)`

Great work on this package. I realize that this is intended to be used with shiny, but it works excellently without it with one exception: 

If you run enough queries using the pool, eventually you'll get one or more of the following error: 

`Error: could not find function "task"`

```
# ... other irrelevant stuff
13: poolReturn(con)
14: pool$release(object)
15: naiveScheduler$protect({
       pool_metadata <- attr(object, "pool_metadata", exact = TRUE)
       if (pool_metadata$state == "free") {
           stop("This object was already returned to the pool.")
       }
       if (is.null(pool_metadata) || !pool_metadata$valid) {
           stop("Invalid object.")
       }
       if (!self$valid) {
           private$changeObjectStatus(object, NULL)
           return()
       }
       tryCatch({
           onPassivate(object)
       }, error = function(e) {
           private$changeObjectStatus(object, NULL)
           stop("Object could not be returned back to the pool. ", "It was destroyed instead. Error message: ", conditionMessage(e))
       })
       taskHandle <- scheduleTask(self$idleTimeout, function() {
           if (self$counters$free + self$counters$taken > self$minSize) {
               private$changeObjectStatus(object, NULL)
           }
       })
       pool_metadata$destroyHandle <- taskHandle
       private$changeObjectStatus(object, "free")
       if (!(is.null(getOption("pool.scheduler", NULL)))) {
           pool_metadata$validateHandle <- scheduleTaskRecurring(self$validationInterval, function() {
               object <- private$checkValid(object)
               onPassivate(object)
           })
       }
   })
16: private$refCount$release()
17: private$callback()
18: self$protect({
       task()
   })
19: force(expr)
```

I can't seem to find `task()` in this repo, so I presume that it's something in shiny, because if I run the same code over (a series of db pull tests that are now using pool). I get this error if I haven't called `library(shiny)` or treated it as a package dependency.

It would be great to see this as a standalone package that doesn't require `shiny`.

This is the bare bones pool package. 

_Stuff that I'm still working on:_
- wrapping all (most really) DBI methods for Pool instances;
- making the Pool class robust (need to incorporate a lot more error handling and thinking of edge cases);
- implement a finalizer to check for and destroy leaked connections;
- implement a timeout mechanism to destroy leaked connections after an x amount of time and also to gradually shrink the pool size if connections have been idle for too long;
- better documentation for methods (the reason why the Travis check is failing at the moment).

_Open questions:_
- should `createPool` be a complete abstract generic? Or should it be implemented for each pooled object type (ex: `createDBIPool`, `createMongoPool`, `createBufferPool`)? The former might seem more elegant, but the latter would allow the generic to specify the actual arguments, with descriptive names, needed for the creation of each type of pool (ex: `drv` for DBI, and maybe something else like a factory function for other object types) -- instead of relying of vague argument names and `...` for everything else.

cc @jcheng5, @wch, @hadley
